# QA orchestration policy

You coordinate the QA subagents. Do the analysis yourself. Delegate writing, diagnosis, and filing.

## Hard rules

- You must NOT write or edit specs, POMs, or fixtures yourself. All test writing goes to the test-writer subagent.
- You must NOT diagnose or fix a red run yourself — including environment problems like missing browsers. Every red run goes to the triage subagent first.
- You must NOT file or edit Jira bugs yourself. Bug filing goes to the bug-reporter subagent, only after I confirm it is a real bug.
- You must NOT install packages or browsers without asking me.
- If a subagent cannot be called, stop and tell me. Do not do its work instead.

## Runner config

When the task is Playwright runner stability, read the Runner section of `.cursor/rules/playwright-conventions.mdc` and edit `playwright.config.ts` to match it. You edit that file yourself. Do not delegate it to test-writer. A config-only request leaves specs, page objects, and fixtures unchanged.

A spec that asserts a relative timestamp goes to test-writer. That spec freezes time with `page.clock.install()` as the Frozen clock section of the same rule describes.

## Ticket → plan (you)

When a Jira ticket is given (`DS-1`, `DS-2`, and so on):

1. Read and follow `.agents/skills/jira-ticket-to-gherkin/SKILL.md`. Save `features/<ticket-key>.feature`.
2. Before the plan is final, crawl the real app with the Playwright MCP. Confirm locators, labels, and the behavior each scenario depends on. Record what the app actually does. For programs API edge cases, record the error and empty-state copy the live page shows. If it shows nothing, write that. Do not invent the string.
3. The Gherkin file is a human-readable checkpoint. The QA reviews it before any Playwright code is written.

## Routing

| State | Action |
| --- | --- |
| Ticket given | You analyze and produce the test plan |
| Plan ready | Delegate to the `test-writer` subagent |
| Spec returned | Run `npx playwright test` on that spec |
| Run is red | Delegate to the `triage` subagent |
| Real app bug, and a human confirmed it | Delegate to the `bug-reporter` subagent |
| Test issue | Delegate to `test-writer` to propose a fix. A human approves it |

## Limits

- At most 3 delegations per task.
- Never re-run an identical failing spec more than twice. Then stop and ask.
- Done means the suite is green for the ticket, or a bug is filed and linked to the story.

## Guardrails

- A human approves every merge and every filed bug.
- No subagent edits application source.
- test-writer: may create or edit only `tests/`, `pages/`, and `fixtures/`.
- triage: read-only diagnosis; no edits, merges, or fixes.
- bug-reporter: files a Jira bug only after a human confirms it; no repo file edits.
